---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

export const prerender = false;
---

<BaseLayout title="Services — DARKROOM">
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="pt-10 md:pt-16">
    <h1 class="text-[52px] leading-[0.88] tracking-wide sm:text-[72px] md:text-[96px]">
      Our Services
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-7 md:text-base" style="color: var(--muted);">
      We sell cameras and film, develop and scan your rolls, colour grade your scans, and rent
      out cameras and other photography equipment - everything you need, start to finish.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Service details -->
  <section class="mt-16 md:mt-24 max-w-3xl">
    <div class="space-y-0">

      <div class="service-item py-10 border-t" style="border-color: var(--line);">
        <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-wide">Cameras</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
          We carry a range of cameras to suit every level, from disposable and point-and-shoot to fully manual SLRs.
          Whether you are picking up a camera for the first time or looking for a specific body, we can help you find the right fit.
        </p>
        <details class="mt-4 group">
          <summary class="read-more-btn">Read more</summary>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Disposable cameras</strong> are the simplest way to get started.
              Load nothing, set nothing, just shoot. They are ideal for events, travel, or handing to a friend
              who has never touched film. Once you are done, hand it back to us and we will develop and scan it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Point-and-shoot cameras</strong> sit in the middle ground.
              Compact, automatic, and easy to carry anywhere. You do not have to think about exposure,
              but you still get the warmth and texture that film is known for. Popular models include
              the Olympus Stylus series and the Canon Sure Shot.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">SLR cameras</strong> give you full control. Set your aperture,
              pick your shutter speed, change your lens. They are bigger and require more thought, but that is
              part of the appeal. Classic bodies like the Minolta X-700, Canon AE-1, and Nikon FM2 are some
              of the most loved cameras ever made.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="service-item py-10 border-t" style="border-color: var(--line);">
        <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-wide">Film</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
          We stock the most popular films for everyday shooting, professional work, and creative experimentation.
          Kodak Gold 200, Portra 400, Lomography Redscale, and more.
        </p>
        <details class="mt-4 group">
          <summary class="read-more-btn">Read more</summary>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Color negative film (C-41)</strong> is what most people
              think of when they think of film. It is forgiving on exposure, produces rich colors, and is
              the most widely available. Kodak Gold 200 is a great all-rounder for daylight shooting.
              Kodak Ultramax 400 handles lower light well. Portra 400 is the professional choice,
              with fine grain and beautiful skin tones.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Black and white film</strong> gives your images a completely
              different character. Ilford HP5 Plus is versatile and handles pushing well. Kodak Tri-X has a
              grittier, more contrasty look that many photographers swear by. Both can be shot in any light
              and developed here.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Experimental film</strong> is for when you want results
              you cannot fully predict. Lomography Redscale is shot through the back of the film, shifting
              everything toward deep reds and oranges. Expired film can produce grain, color shifts, and
              light leaks that are hard to replicate digitally. Part of the fun is not knowing exactly
              what you will get.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="service-item py-10 border-t" style="border-color: var(--line);">
        <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-wide">Developing</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
          We develop C-41 color negative and black and white film in-house.
          Drop off your rolls and we will take it from there.
        </p>
        <details class="mt-4 group">
          <summary class="read-more-btn">Read more</summary>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
            <p>
              Film developing is a chemical process that turns your exposed roll into a set of negatives.
              The film goes through a series of chemical baths at precise temperatures and timings.
              Too hot, too cold, or too long in any step and the results suffer. We do this carefully, every time.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">C-41 color film</strong> is the standard process for
              most color negative films. You will get consistent, clean negatives ready for scanning.
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Black and white</strong> developing has more variables,
              and if you have specific preferences around developer or contrast, we are happy to discuss that with you.
            </p>
            <p>
              Turnaround time depends on how many rolls we have in at any given time. We will always give
              you an estimate when you drop off, and we will reach out as soon as your film is ready.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="service-item py-10 border-t" style="border-color: var(--line);">
        <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-wide">Scanning</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
          Developing gives you negatives. Scanning turns those negatives into digital files you can
          actually use, share, and print.
        </p>
        <details class="mt-4 group">
          <summary class="read-more-btn">Read more</summary>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
            <p>
              After your film is developed, we scan each frame at high resolution. The scan is what
              you will share, post, and print, so the quality of it matters just as much as the
              quality of the shot.
            </p>
            <p>
              We scan at resolutions suited for large prints, not just small social media thumbnails.
              Files are delivered digitally as high-quality JPEGs. If you need something specific,
              like TIFFs for printing or a particular resolution, just let us know when you drop off.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you have a specific use in mind, whether that is a large format print or Instagram posts,
              let us know and we will make sure the scan is suited for it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="service-item py-10 border-t border-b" style="border-color: var(--line);">
        <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-wide">Color Grading</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
          A standard scan shows your film as it is. Color grading takes it further, shaping the
          tones, contrast, and colors to get a specific look.
        </p>
        <details class="mt-4 group">
          <summary class="read-more-btn">Read more</summary>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-7" style="color: var(--muted);">
            <p>
              When a negative is scanned, the scanner makes basic decisions about how to interpret
              the colors. A standard scan is accurate, but accurate does not always mean it looks
              the way you imagined it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Color grading lets us shape the mood of your images. A warm, faded look with lifted
              shadows. Deep, punchy contrast with rich blacks. Cooler highlights to give a clean,
              editorial feel. The direction depends on the film you shot and what you are after.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you have reference images or a specific aesthetic in mind, share them with us when
              you drop off. The more specific you are, the closer we can get.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How it works -->
  <section class="mt-24 md:mt-32">
    <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl leading-[0.95] tracking-wide">How it works</h2>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      {[
        { n: "01", title: "Order online", body: "Add your services to the cart and submit your order. You'll receive a confirmation invoice." },
        { n: "02", title: "Drop off your film", body: "We'll contact you on your preferred messaging app to arrange a convenient drop-off time." },
        { n: "03", title: "Pick up your scans", body: "Once processed, we'll notify you. Your digital scans will be delivered or ready for collection." },
      ].map(step => (
        <div class="rounded-3xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--panel)] p-8">
          <div class="font-mono text-4xl" style="color: var(--red);">{step.n}</div>
          <div class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold">{step.title}</div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6" style="color: var(--muted);">{step.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="mt-24 mb-10 rounded-3xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--panel)] p-10 md:p-16 text-center">
    <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl leading-[0.95] tracking-wide">Ready to shoot?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm" style="color: var(--muted);">Browse the full service menu and add rolls to your cart.</p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex justify-center gap-4 flex-wrap">
      <a
        href="/shop"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-[999px] bg-[var(--btn)] px-10 py-3 text-base font-medium text-[var(--btnText)] hover:opacity-90"
      >
        Shop
      </a>
      <a
        href="/about#contact"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-[999px] border border-[var(--line)] px-10 py-3 text-base font-medium hover:bg-[var(--panel)]"
      >
        Contact us
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    details summary {
      list-style: none;
    }
    details summary::-webkit-details-marker {
      display: none;
    }

    .read-more-btn {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 6px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-size: 12px;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
      transition: color 0.2s ease;
      user-select: none;
    }
    .read-more-btn:hover {
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .read-more-btn::after {
      content: "↓";
      font-size: 11px;
      transition: transform 0.2s ease;
    }
    details[open] .read-more-btn::after {
      transform: rotate(180deg);
    }
    details[open] .read-more-btn {
      color: var(--text);
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
